";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:1;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:19:"The Ocean - Solaris";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:247:"It all builds up to something much more exciting than just a retread. Rather, it's a rethink, an approach to the band's past that insists not just on regurgitation but a reprocessing that results in a new approach towards the same goal and sound. ";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:62:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/25/the-ocean-solaris/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6ab5e13bfb887b0001396570";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:9:"The Ocean";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Fri, 25 Sep 2026 14:00:43 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:15:"Eden Kupermintz";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:111:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/a1859429010_10.jpg";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:4675:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/a1859429010_10.jpg" alt="The Ocean - Solaris"><p>I didn&apos;t really like <em>Holocene</em>. I could, however, <em>appreciate </em>it. That distinction is important: while the album wasn&apos;t for me, I could see what <strong>The Ocean</strong><em> </em>were trying to do with it. More than that, I accepted that experimentation and change was the entire point of the project. Hell, <em>Pelagial</em> (still my favorite album from the band) is built around the very idea of change and transformation. But the direction the band was going in wasn&apos;t for me; I didn&apos;t think they executed it with enough skill to hold my interest. And so, I approached <em>Solaris</em> with some trepidation. I knew what to expect, namely something different, especially when I heard that <em>four</em> new band members were being added. What form would The Ocean take this time? Would some of the &quot;old&quot; sound return and, more importantly I think, did I want it to?</p><p>Well, the answer to both of those questions is &quot;sort of&quot;. <em>Solaris</em> is definitely more akin to <em>Pelagial </em>and <em>Phanerozoic</em> than <em>Holocene</em> was. It has plenty of electronic sounds and touches to it but it very much revolves around some of the tried and true elements of The Ocean, namely post-metal built around a concept album. But there are also differences. As you might be able to tell from looking at the cover art, <em>Solaris</em> is a &quot;brighter&quot; album. What do I mean by that? First, the compositions are less organized around crushing force and more around exploration and melody. Even <em>Phanerozoic</em>, which wasn&apos;t explicitly built around the concept of weight like <em>Pelagial </em>was, used its instruments to oppress and crush. But <em>Solaris</em> has a whole gamut of new sounds; check out &quot;Light Pollution&quot; for example and the undulating, mercurial segment around the four minute mark and <em>especially</em> the exquisite guitar bridge around four minutes and fifty seconds.</p><p>Have you really heard The Ocean do this in the past? Not quite. And while you&apos;re there, you probably picked up on the vocals of Lane Shi (<strong>Elizabeth Color Wheel</strong>, <strong>F&#xf3;rn</strong>) who is by far the biggest new addition to this album. The Loic Rosetti style vocals, which Enrico Tiberi does an uncanny job of recreating,<strong> </strong>really benefit from having a counter-part on this release. It&apos;s not that Tiberi&apos;s voice is boring. But the interaction between his timbre and the rest of The Ocean&apos;s instruments is tried and true. Shi, on the other hand, brings a whole new texture and palette to the record, creating much of that &quot;brightness&quot; I was referring to earlier. Whether screaming, vocalizing, humming, or emoting she&apos;s a breath of fresh air to the record and my favorite change on it by far. Other changes include those I&apos;ve already highlighted, like new ideas from new guitarists, new ideas for how tracks are &quot;stitched&quot; together, like the bells on &quot;Simulacra&quot; or the overall approach to leitmotifs and guitar riffs on the album.</p><p>Speaking about &quot;Simulacra&quot;, holy shit do I <em>love</em> this track. It&apos;s perhaps my favorit example of what <em>Solaris</em> does so well. It&apos;s <em>definitely</em> The Ocean but it also sounds so different, the chords chosen, the structure of the track, the synths, even what Staps does with his singing on this track. It builds up to something much more exciting than just a retread. Rather, it&apos;s a re<em>think</em>, an approach to the band&apos;s past that insists not just on regurgitation but a reprocessing that results in a new approach towards the same goal and sound. </p><p>Which brings me back to the question of did I want the &quot;old&quot; The Ocean back and my answer of &quot;sort of&quot;. I am very happy to hear the heaviness come back to the foreground and The Ocean back to what they do well: creating meticulously constructed, concept heavy post-metal. But I am also equally happy that they <em>did not</em> go back to <em>Pelagial</em> or <em>Phanerozoic</em><u>,</u> as much as I love those releases. I am extremely glad that they held on to what I think makes them unique and yes, what sometimes leads them to make releases I don&apos;t enjoy as much: their fierce dedication to change. You never know what The Ocean will do next and they have proven this once again on <em>Solaris</em>. Thankfully, the end result is also something I love. </p>";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:2;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"Release Roundup: 9/25/26";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:239:"Rounding up new releases from The Ocean, Godflesh, Europe, Tom Morello, Gåte, Burner, Critical Defiance, Lacrimas Profundere, Crown Magnetar, Pure Reason Revolution, Scorpion Milk, Ordeathral, Slaughtercult, Yarotz, Brat, Barren and more.";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:68:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/25/release-roundup-9-25-26/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6ab3237c99796a0001970724";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:3:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:19:"Release Day Roundup";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:9:"The Ocean";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:28:"Uncle Acid And The Deadbeats";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Fri, 25 Sep 2026 12:00:27 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:14:"Joshua Bulleid";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:129:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/1916512-belligerence-pt-i_053355.png";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:31326:"<h2 id="top-pick">Top Pick</h2><h3 id="the-ocean-%E2%80%93-solaris-progressive-post-metal">The Ocean &#x2013; <em>Solaris</em> (progressive post-metal) </h3><img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/1916512-belligerence-pt-i_053355.png" alt="Release Roundup: 9/25/26"><p>Eden should be along with a proper review sometime in the near future, but it&apos;s safe to say this first outing from the new <strong>Ocean l</strong>ine-up is not only a bona fide success, but also stands out as one of their strongest albums to date. The jump in quality might not come as a surprise after the snoozefest that was <em>Holoxene </em>(2023), but there&apos;s an argument to be made that this is the best Ocean album since genuine GOAT-contender <em>Pelagial </em>(2013), or even outside of it.</p><p>At well over an hour and with songs and song-suites pushing the ten, fifteen and even twenty-minute mark, <em>Solaris </em>can be a tall order. Likewise, its expansive and layered composition works best when played in full and paying it complete attention, which is probably why I haven&apos;t listened to it nearly as much as I should have, even though we&apos;ve had the promo for a month or so now. Rest assured though, that this is an album I will be playing and referring back to for years to come, and I doubt I&apos;m alone in that assessment.</p>
