<p>Wintering Grounds &#x2013; <a href="https://winteringgrounds.bandcamp.com/album/impasse?from=search&amp;search_item_id=1638995777&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362326&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=3a3996e5e1944155c356db91ef417b9d&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Impasse</a> (progressive death metal)</p><p>Worldsucks &#x2013; <a href="https://worldsucks.bandcamp.com/album/scorn-for-the-u-s-a?from=search&amp;search_item_id=611836009&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362487&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=2fb4432a88dbf10fe367a5d7ddaca506&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Scorn for the U.S.A.</a> (blackened grind)</p><p>Xion &#x2013; <a href="https://ff-xion.bandcamp.com/album/when-stars-collide?from=search&amp;search_item_id=3944243339&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362040&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=745d1b4729c92a5b31b26341eb137b18&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">When Stars Collide</a> (heavy metal, thrash)</p>";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:11;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:25:"Green Lung - Necropolitan";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:225:"what's on this album is very good - no one will catch me skipping a Green Lung track when I want to rock the fuck out. But sometimes I want other things and Green Lung's previous albums gave that to me. Necropolitan does not.";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:68:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/10/green-lung-necropolitan/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6aa20da5d3263100011dcbc3";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:10:"Green Lung";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:00:19 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:15:"Eden Kupermintz";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:103:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/422469.jpg";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:3681:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/422469.jpg" alt="Green Lung - Necropolitan"><p>If you ask me (and you are, since you&apos;re reading this review), the power of <strong>Green Lung</strong> always lay in the tension between their rock n&apos; roll groove and their power ballad delivery. For me, neither part was strong enough to stand on its own; rather, the way each set up the other was what made Green Lung so good. Moving from &quot;Hunters in the Sky&quot; into &quot;Oceans of Time&quot; for example (both from <em>This Heathen Land</em>) just gets me every time. The former is ripping and roaring track arranged around a monstrously infectious chorus while the latter is a gothic, bombastic, and flamboyant tribute to one of the best divas to ever do it, Count Vlad Dracula himself. It&apos;s the tension and the payoff that keeps me coming back again and again to the album and, indeed, to Green Lung&apos;s entire backlog.</p><p>Maybe you disagree; maybe you love Green Lung simply for their ability to conjure the spirit of rock n&apos; roll at their tip of their guitars and infuse it with <em>just</em> enough stoner metal magic. I know at least one person like this and while I disagree, I respect the position; there is no doubt that Green Lung rock. If that&apos;s what you&apos;re after, then <em>Necropolitan</em>, their upcoming release, has it in spades. From the big main riff of &quot;Black Magic Radio&quot; to the soaring solo and vocals of &quot;Together in Death&quot; (some of the best from the band on both accounts), <em>Necropolitan </em>is all rock. There&apos;s a touch of the power ballad on tracks like &quot;To the Gallows Born&quot;, which closes the album, and even some at the beginning of the aforementioned &quot;Together in Death&quot;. But that&apos;s about it; the rest is dedicated to the worship of the riff.</p><p>And, for me, this is also where the album&apos;s weakness lies. I found myself getting a bit lost in the sea of licks, grooves, and ear-worm targeted choruses. When I tuned in, everything I heard was good, even very good. But it was hard to tune in, as the mode of the album maintained the same pace, the same vibe, and the same intent. I longed for the contrast; the first two tracks went by and I headbanged and danced and had fun but then I wanted to be a bit wistful, you know? To raise my arms in praise of a higher power. To dream of the trees. Green Lung stuff, you know? And it was completely absent. </p><p>There are so many examples from Green Lung&apos;s career that I could add to the ones in the first paragraph - &quot;May Queen&quot;, &quot;One For Sorrow&quot;, &quot;Graveyard Sun&quot;. These are some of Green Lung&apos;s best and most memorable tracks, exactly because they temper the hot, sharp steel of Green Lung&apos;s groove with a colder, more epic, and more grandiose iron that, in my opinion, is sorely needed. However, as I said, what&apos;s on this album is very good - no one will catch me skipping a Green Lung track when the window is down and the car is going fast and I want to rock the fuck out. But sometimes I want other things and Green Lung&apos;s previous albums gave that to me. <em>Necropolitan</em> does not. Alas!</p>
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->
<iframe style="border: 0; width: 100%; height: 120px;" src="https://bandcamp.com/EmbeddedPlayer/album=3753026535/size=large/bgcol=ffffff/linkcol=0687f5/tracklist=false/artwork=small/transparent=true/" seamless><a href="https://greenlung.bandcamp.com/album/necropolitan-2?ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Necropolitan by GREEN LUNG</a></iframe>
<!--kg-card-end: html-->
";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:12;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:45:"Dreadnought - Wars of Spirit// Wards of Light";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:129:" Wars of Spirit // Wards of Light is a further step in the band's career, deeper into the beating, shadowed heart of their sound.";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:86:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/09/dreadnought-wars-of-spirit-wards-of-light/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6aa0c56990cd940001dc2d88";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:11:"Dreadnought";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Wed, 09 Sep 2026 14:00:28 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:15:"Eden Kupermintz";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:147:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/cover-Dreadnought---Wars-Of-Spirit-Wards-Of-Light-.jpg";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:4189:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/cover-Dreadnought---Wars-Of-Spirit-Wards-Of-Light-.jpg" alt="Dreadnought - Wars of Spirit// Wards of Light"><p>I&apos;ve read other reviews of <strong>Dreadnought</strong>&apos;s upcoming release and they&apos;ve described the album as more &quot;direct&quot;. I don&apos;t know if I necessarily agree or, rather, if I think that&apos;s the main that should be highlighted. To be sure, <em>Wars of Spirit // Wards of Light</em> (from here on called <em>Wars</em>) <em>is</em> more direct in the sense that the more ethereal passages of previous Dreadnought releases are mostly gone. All tracks clock in under ten minutes and their structure is a bit more straight forward in how it balances the psychedelic, &quot;lighter&quot; elements of Dreadnought&apos;s sound and the more vociferous, black metal sound.</p><p>But there&apos;s a deeper transformation for the band&apos;s sound on <em>Wars</em> that I think merits focus and that&apos;s a sea change in the entire <em>palette</em> of Dreadnought on this release. On previous releases, all the way back to <em>Bridging Realms</em>, Dreadnought lived on a tight rope; the band were constantly balancing those two modes I referenced above. <em>Emergence</em> was perhaps the exception and therefore the album which sounds the most like <em>Wars</em>; like it, <em>Emergence </em>had a more rumbling, bass-forward sound and tended to &quot;explode&quot; less into that other, treble and tenor focused mode of Dreadnought. <em>Wars</em> is very much the same, more contemplative, &quot;richer&quot;, less prone to far flung gestures, and more dedicated to a thicker, more present sort of approach.</p><p>Which is not to say it never explodes; it does. But check out the <em>way</em> in which it does, on opening track &quot;Tiamat&apos;s Gate&quot; five minute mark. In the past, this passage (the catharsis of the track) would have been accompanied by high-pitched screams, whirling float, and punctual piano. But here, the center of the weight is focused on a maelstrom like guitar riff, embellished by deep, sonorous riffs and, of course, Kelly Schilling&apos;s powerful vocals. But they are more of the &quot;haggard&quot; variety, with that unique timbre she has. <em>And</em> they trade places with a much deeper growl provided by Ryan Sims. The end result is a darker, more brooding sound. Yes, Schilling&apos;s familiar clean vocals come in later and yes, the track winds into a soaring peak. But then it&apos;s also immediately snatched into a <em>gorgeous</em>, muted passage punctuated by excellently restrained blast beats, darkly scintillating synths, and deep, subtle bass.</p><p>This new iteration on the sound continues through the release: the synths sound fuller but also more contemplative and atmospheric for example. You can hear that on &quot;Aeonwar&quot;, where they basically good full darkwave in their sound (beautifully melded with Schilling&apos;s gothic style). Likewise the guitars continue to be denser and thicker than before, less worried about tracing every peak and crescendo and more focused on creating texture and weight to the sound. It&apos;s on full display for &quot;Aeonwar&quot;, with one of my favorite riffs from the band&apos;s history doing wonders &quot;beneath&quot; the gossamer synths. It&apos;s Dreadnought, for sure, and it&apos;s made up of the elements which first caused me to fall in love with the band. But most importantly, it champions the quality which I love Dreadnought the most for: change. <em>Wars of Spirit // Wards of Light </em>is a further step in the band&apos;s career, deeper into the beating, shadowed heart of their sound.</p>
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->
